+# Event trajectory recording
+
+Nexent can optionally persist an agent run as a Perix Event v0 trajectory. The
+integration runs entirely inside the Python process: it does not require a Node
+server, sidecar, or TypeScript runtime.
+
+## Install
+
+The `event` extra is pinned to the exact Perix Event source revision used by
+this integration:
+
+```bash
+uv pip install './sdk[event]'
+```
+
+The current pin is `perix-event-sdk` 0.1.0 from Perix runtime data commit
+`2eea3f17e6a917ef3d640405b360664728d31e84`. Production deployments may install
+the wheel built from that revision instead, as long as they keep the same
+package version and Event v0 format.
+
+## Record a run
+
+Pass an explicit `EventTrajectoryConfig` when constructing `AgentRunInfo`:
+
+```python
+from nexent.core.agents import AgentRunInfo, EventTrajectoryConfig
+
+run_info = AgentRunInfo(
+    # Existing AgentRunInfo fields are omitted here.
+    event_trajectory=EventTrajectoryConfig(
+        root="/var/lib/nexent/events",
+        session_id="tenant-user-conversation",
+        cwd="/srv/nexent/workspaces/tenant-user-conversation",
+        compression="none",
+    ),
+)
+```
+
+`root`, `session_id`, and `cwd` are caller-owned stable identities. The SDK
+does not read environment variables. Use a tenant-qualified session id and a
+persistent root shared by the processes that may resume the conversation.
+Both `root` and `cwd` must be absolute. A resumed session must use the same
+`cwd` recorded in its header. Keep the selected compression (`none` or `zstd`)
+unchanged for a root.
+
+`agent_run_thread()` acquires one process-level writer lease, creates or
+resumes the configured Session, records the root agent's turn and real
+model/tool boundaries, flushes every Event before the corresponding side
+effect, and closes the Session when the worker exits. A concurrent writer for
+the same session id is rejected. Managed agents do not create a second writer
+for the root Session.
+
+## Inspect, resume, and fork
+
+Use the native Python Event package after the Nexent run has released its
+writer lease:
+
+```python
+from perix_event import JsonlSessionPersistence, SessionStore
+
+persistence = JsonlSessionPersistence(
+    "/var/lib/nexent/events",
+    compression="none",
+)
+
+# Read-only inspection does not publish a live Session.
+inspection = persistence.inspect("tenant-user-conversation")
+
+# restore() and resume() are equivalent live-session entry points.
+store = SessionStore(persistence)
+session = store.resume("tenant-user-conversation")
+child = store.fork(
+    session,
+    child_session_id="tenant-user-conversation-fork",
+)
+store.flush(child)
+store.close()
+```
+
+Do not open a second `SessionStore` writer while Nexent is recording the same
+Session. Cold restore repairs an interrupted open turn according to Event v0;
+fork accepts only a stable boundary outside an open turn. The resulting JSONL
+artifacts are the common Perix Event format and can be restored directly by the
+TypeScript SDK and rendered by the Trajectory UI.
+
+## Verification
+
+Install both optional test groups and run the focused acceptance suite:
+
+```bash
+uv pip install -e './sdk[event,quality]'
+python -m pytest test/sdk/core/agents/test_event_trajectory.py -q
+python -m pytest test/sdk/core/agents/test_core_agent.py -q
+python -m pytest test/sdk/core/agents/test_run_agent.py -q
+python -m pytest test/sdk/core/agents/test_nexent_agent.py -q
+python -m pytest test/sdk/core/agents/test_agent_model.py -q
+```
+
+Run each file in its own pytest process, matching Nexent's repository test
+runner. The acceptance test launches fresh Python processes from outside the
+repository root, verifies that `perix_event` resolves from `site-packages`,
+executes a real `CoreAgent` tool call, resumes the parent Session, forks it, and
+independently continues the child.
